02. VISUAL DIRECTION: TECHNICAL EDITORIALISM

Develop an original visual language called Technical Editorialism.

The direction combines:

* The precision of a professional code editor.
* The information density of an engineering observability platform.
* The clarity of an incident-investigation interface.
* The visual hierarchy of a carefully typeset technical publication.
* The responsiveness and finish of a premium desktop application.

The result must feel like a purpose-built product rather than a collection of components selected from a UI library.

Use deliberate contrast between typography, code surfaces, information panels, and supporting metadata.

Allow technical information to establish the visual identity.

Use real source code, structured findings, meaningful identifiers, and precise technical labels as part of the interface itself.

Do not fill empty areas with arbitrary graphics.

Do not use generic AI illustrations.

Do not use glowing brains, floating robots, meaningless network diagrams, or decorative representations of intelligence.

Do not use a full-screen gradient as a substitute for a visual identity.

Do not cover the application in glassmorphism.

Do not place every element inside a rounded card.

Do not create the familiar generic SaaS layout consisting of a sidebar, four identical metric cards, and a large chart.

Instead, establish a recognisable visual language through typography, geometry, surface treatments, information density, and the way the application presents evidence.

The result should remain visually coherent even when all decorative elements are removed.

2.1 Colour system

Create a dark-first design system with a carefully controlled palette.

Use:

* Deep graphite for the application canvas.
* Layered charcoal for major workspaces.
* Slightly lighter surfaces for active panels.
* Warm off-white for primary text.
* Muted cool gray for secondary information.
* Restrained chartreuse for primary actions and important active states.
* Muted blue for navigation, technical references, and informational states.
* Amber for warnings.
* Red for serious defects.
* Green for successful operations and validated positive states.

Use semantic design tokens.

Define tokens for:

* Application backgrounds.
* Raised surfaces.
* Interactive surfaces.
* Borders.
* Text hierarchy.
* Focus rings.
* Severity levels.
* Success, warning, and error states.
* Selection and hover.
* Charts and visualisations.

Do not use the accent colour everywhere.

Use it to establish hierarchy and guide attention.

The interface must not look like a neon gaming dashboard.

Avoid unnecessary outer glows, saturated gradients, excessive shadows, and decorative outlines.

Use subtle tonal differences to create depth.

Borders should be visible enough to establish structure without making the interface resemble a spreadsheet.

Provide a complete light theme with independently considered contrast and surface hierarchy.

Do not simply invert the dark theme.

Persist the selected theme.

Dark mode should be the initial default.

2.2 Typography

Typography is a primary design element.

Choose a highly legible sans-serif typeface for the interface and a professional monospace typeface for source code, identifiers, line numbers, technical metadata, and logs.

Use typography to distinguish:

* Page-level context.
* Major findings.
* Technical explanations.
* Secondary metadata.
* Source-code evidence.
* System status.
* Interactive controls.

Use compact, carefully spaced headings rather than oversized marketing typography.

Use tabular numerals where numbers need to align.

Ensure source-code line numbers are visually distinct from source content.

Use italics, uppercase labels, or condensed metadata sparingly.

Never reduce technical information to unreadable text just to increase apparent sophistication.

2.3 Geometry and composition

Establish a consistent spacing and sizing system.

Use deliberate relationships between:

* Navigation.
* Workspaces.
* Source-code surfaces.
* Findings.
* Supporting panels.
* Dialogs.
* Tables.
* Technical metadata.

Avoid arbitrary differences in padding and border radius.

Use square or subtly rounded technical surfaces where appropriate.

Reserve larger radii for the few places where they serve a clear interaction or compositional purpose.

Allow some sections to use open layouts without enclosing every element.

Use alignment, whitespace, dividers, and surface contrast to organise information.

Do not force the same layout onto every screen.

A review workspace and a settings page should not look structurally identical.

