03. THE SIGNATURE EXPERIENCE: THE REVIEW WORKSPACE

This is the most important part of PatchGuard AI.

The main review workspace must be compelling enough that a developer would genuinely want to spend time using it.

Do not make the primary experience a form that produces a list of AI suggestions.

Build a sophisticated code investigation environment.

3.1 Workspace composition

On desktop, use a carefully balanced, resizable multi-panel layout.

The primary workspace should contain three functional regions:

A. SOURCE AND CHANGE CONTEXT

B. FINDINGS AND REVIEW SUMMARY

C. SELECTED FINDING DETAILS AND EVIDENCE

The precise proportions may vary based on the screen width and the selected mode.

The source and findings areas should receive the majority of the available space.

The detail panel should provide enough width to read explanations and remediation without forcing excessive wrapping.

The layout must feel deliberate at common laptop resolutions.

Support resizing where practical.

If panels can be resized, implement the interaction correctly and persist useful layout preferences when appropriate.

Provide sensible minimum widths.

Prevent panels from collapsing into unusable slivers.

When the viewport is too narrow for three columns, transition into a responsive arrangement rather than shrinking every panel.

The mobile experience should preserve the essential workflow, even if the desktop composition cannot be reproduced exactly.

3.2 Source navigation

Provide a clear source-navigation region when a review contains multiple files.

Display:

* Filename.
* File extension or language.
* Change status where applicable.
* Finding count for that file.
* Relevant review status.

Use meaningful indicators rather than decorative icons.

Support selecting a file to update the source viewer.

Do not display fabricated repository files when the user has only submitted a single code snippet.

If repository integration has not been implemented, use the available source input to populate the workspace.

If multiple files are supported later, design the data model so the interface can accommodate them without being rebuilt.

3.3 Source viewer

Use an established code-editor or syntax-highlighting component where appropriate.

Prefer a reliable, maintainable editor integration over a custom imitation of a complete IDE.

The source viewer should support:

* Syntax highlighting.
* Line numbers.
* Source selection where useful.
* Distinct active-line styling.
* Finding markers in the gutter where supported.
* Highlighting the selected finding’s relevant lines.
* Long-line handling.
* Clear separation between source and annotations.
* Readable code typography.
* Keyboard accessibility.

When a finding is selected, navigate to its relevant source location if that location is valid.

Keep the selected finding visually connected to the highlighted code.

If a finding references a line that does not exist, do not highlight a nearby line.

Display the location as unavailable or invalid and record the inconsistency where appropriate.

When a source file is too long to render efficiently, use appropriate virtualisation or another scalable approach.

Do not render an enormous source file in a way that freezes the interface.

3.4 Finding markers

Introduce a restrained system of source annotations.

Where supported, display finding markers next to the relevant lines.

Markers should communicate the presence and severity of a finding without overwhelming the code.

When multiple findings refer to the same line, aggregate their markers sensibly.

Clicking a marker should select the associated finding or reveal the findings attached to that line.

Hovering should provide a short accessible summary.

Do not use hover as the only way to access important information.

Provide keyboard-accessible alternatives.

Do not introduce source markers unless the source viewer can map them to actual finding locations.

