09. EVALUATION LAB: THE MOST IMPORTANT ADVANCED SCREEN

Design a dedicated Evaluation Lab for testing the reliability of PatchGuard AI.

This screen is important because the product should demonstrate engineering discipline rather than relying on attractive AI-generated findings.

Do not present benchmark results until actual evaluation runs have produced them.

The Evaluation Lab should eventually support:

* Curated test cases.
* Expected findings.
* Actual findings.
* Precision.
* Recall.
* False-positive analysis.
* Detection by severity.
* Detection by category.
* Review latency.
* Model and configuration comparisons.

For the initial implementation, create the actual interface and data contracts needed to support evaluation.

Implement only the evaluation functionality that can be backed by real data.

If the evaluation engine has not yet been implemented, show a clearly labelled empty state explaining how evaluation will work.

Do not display fabricated benchmark charts.

9.1 Evaluation layout

Use a different visual composition from the main dashboard.

Consider:

* A test-suite navigation panel.
* A central results table.
* A selected test-case detail panel.
* A compact metric summary when actual measurements exist.

The screen should communicate that this is a testing and validation environment.

9.2 Test-case details

A test case should eventually be able to show:

* Test identifier.
* Language.
* Bug category.
* Expected result.
* Actual result.
* Whether the result matched expectations.
* Relevant source code.
* Detected findings.
* Execution metadata.

The UI should distinguish a genuine measured result from an unexecuted test case.

Do not mark tests as passed without executing them.

9.3 Metric definitions

Document the meaning of each evaluation metric.

Use appropriate definitions:

Precision = True Positives / (True Positives + False Positives)

Recall = True Positives / (True Positives + False Negatives)

Define how findings are matched to expected defects before calculating these values.

Do not calculate evaluation metrics using arbitrary counts.

Handle zero denominators explicitly.

Do not claim that performance on a small curated dataset proves general reliability.

