10. SYSTEM OBSERVABILITY AND DIAGNOSTICS

A backend engineer should not have to guess why the interface is failing.

Introduce a carefully designed diagnostics experience.

This must not become an uncontrolled log viewer.

The goal is to expose useful operational context without overwhelming the primary workflow.

Provide a compact system-status area showing only states that can actually be verified.

Possible states include:

* Backend reachable.
* AI provider configured.
* Latest request succeeded or failed.
* Static-analysis availability.
* Persistence mode.

Do not report an AI provider as healthy merely because an API key exists.

Distinguish configuration checks from actual connectivity checks.

If a health endpoint exists, use it.

If an operation has not been tested, show an unknown or unverified state.

10.1 Diagnostics details

Where supported, expose:

* Request identifier.
* Review identifier.
* Request duration.
* Analysis stage.
* Error category.
* Retry availability.
* Relevant timestamp.

Do not expose API keys, internal credentials, or sensitive source code through diagnostic metadata.

Do not expose raw stack traces to ordinary users.

A developer diagnostics view may show appropriately sanitised technical details.

10.2 Failure states

Design polished failure experiences for:

* Backend unavailable.
* Invalid request.
* Missing server configuration.
* AI provider rate limit.
* AI provider timeout.
* Invalid model response.
* Persistence failure.
* Incomplete analysis.

Every failure state should explain what happened at an appropriate level of detail and offer a reasonable next action.

Avoid generic messages such as “Something went wrong” when the application knows the failure category.

Do not claim that an error has been resolved until the relevant operation succeeds.

